-- title:
--   Tate point: j(mathsf q^{qℓ'd}) as a cyclic quotient j-invariant
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q\ge 5$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number not divisible by $q$, and $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $A$ (the predicate `LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $(q:\overline{\mathbb Q})\in A.\mathrm{nonunits}$).
--
--   *Reduction data in characteristic $q$.* A finite set $W$ of places of the field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$, the subfield of $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$-Laurent series generated by $j$ and its $M'$-fold $\mathsf q$-substitute, is required by `hW` to consist exactly of the places in $\mathrm{ssPlaces}\,q\,M'\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)$, the supersingular places; $s$ is an element of $W$. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion of intermediate fields $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'\le \mathrm{fieldBar}\,q\,M'$, that is, the $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-base change of the full level-$M'$ modular function field is contained in the $X_H$-function field of level $q^2M'$ for the subgroup $\mathrm{levelH}\,q\,M'$, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$. Further, $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\,(\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$: a valuation subring of integers, a surjective residue homomorphism onto the reduced field whose kernel is the maximal ideal and which is compatible with $A\to\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$ on constants, a scaling clause making every nonzero function reducible to something nonzero, and a degree-preserving map on places compatible with the orders of functions along divisors. The hypothesis `hR₀` says that for every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-Laurent series lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar}\,M'$, that element belongs to $R_0$'s integers and its $R_0$-residue is, as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A$, the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   *The base valuation ring.* $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ an element lying in $A$. Writing $A_0$ for the contraction $A\cap k_0$ (the comap of $A$ along $k_0\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$), the hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` require that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$, that $A_0$ is Henselian local, and that its residue field is algebraically closed; `hκ` requires that every $a\in A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to some element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   *The auxiliary prime and the coefficient field of the $\mathsf q$-expansions.* $\ell'$ is a prime with $\ell'\ne q$, $\ell'\ge 3$ and $\ell'\nmid M'$; $\xi\in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell')$-th root of unity, and `hιξ` requires a ring homomorphism $k_0\to\mathbb C$ carrying $\xi$ to $\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell'))$. The field $K$ is an intermediate field of $k_0\subseteq k_0((\mathsf q))$, fixed by `hK` to be the $k_0$-base change of the $X_H$-function field of level $(q\ell')^2M'$ for the subgroup $\mathrm{levelH}\,(q\ell')\,M'$, the kernel of the reduction $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell')^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell')^\times$; $K$ carries compatible $A_0$- and $k_0$-algebra structures. The element $j\in K$ is nonzero and its image in $k_0((\mathsf q))$ is the coefficient extension of the rational $\mathsf q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$ of the modular $j$-function.
--
--   *Moduli apparatus over $A_0$.* The hypotheses `hℓ` and `hM` are the variable-change equivariance clauses: over any $A_0$-algebra $T$, any Weierstrass curve $W$, variable change $C$ and `LevelPData` $D$, an $\ell'$-level-$P$ structure on $W$ gives one on $C\cdot W$ with $D$ replaced by $D.\mathrm{variableChange}\,C$; and a $\Gamma_0$-power kernel polynomial $h$ for $(p,k)$ on $W$ gives the polynomial $\mathrm{kernelVariableChangeDeg}\,C\,(\mathrm{gamma0PowDeg}\,p\,k)\,h$ for $C\cdot W$. Next, $\mathcal G$ is a family of relative group laws on the projective models of Weierstrass curves over $A_0$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (`h𝒢`) and with origin the identity section (`h𝒢O`), and $\mathcal T$ is a level transport for $\mathcal G$ and $q$, assumed to be a section transport (`h𝒯`). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` provide, for every $A_0$-algebra $T$, every projective Weierstrass curve and every variable change, respectively every $A_0$-algebra map of coefficients, a graded ring homomorphism of the associated graded coordinate rings which dominates the irrelevant ideal and which is a variable-change homomorphism, respectively a coefficient homomorphism.
--
--   Let $\mathcal R=\mathrm{rigidDataPow}\,A_0\,\ell'\,M'\,q\,h\ell\,hM\,\mathcal G\,\mathcal T$, the rigidification of the product of the $\Gamma_0(M')$-power component, the level-$\ell'$ component and the Drinfeld level-$q$ component; a raw point of $\mathcal R$ over $T$ consists of a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant together with level data: for each prime factor $p$ of $M'$ a kernel polynomial, a `LevelPData` for $\ell'$, and a Drinfeld pair of sections for $q$. Then $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for $\mathcal R$'s moduli datum, i.e. an $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ with a point over $B_0$ such that every point over an $A_0$-algebra $T$ is the image of it under a unique $A_0$-algebra map $B_0\to T$; $x_0$ is a point of this datum over $K$ whose $j$-invariant, read in $k_0((\mathsf q))$, is $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,(q\ell')$, the $(q\ell')$-fold $\mathsf q$-substitute of $j$ (hypothesis `hx₀`); and $\mathrm{clC}$ together with `hclC` expresses that the classifying map $P_0.\mathrm{classify}\,x_0:B_0\to K$ factors through the subalgebra $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A_0\,K\,j$ of elements of $K$ integral over $A_0[j]$.
--
--   *The Tate shape of $x_0$ (hypothesis `hμ`).* There exist a variable change $C$ over $k_0((\mathsf q))$ and a raw point $r$ of $\mathcal R$ over $K$ such that: (i) the class of $r$ is $x_0$; (ii) writing $(X_1,Y_1)$ for $\mathrm{cuspPoint}\,k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta\,(1,0)$, the toric point of the Tate curve at the unit $\zeta$ attached to $\xi$, one has $C.u\cdot(2X_1+\tfrac16)=2Y_1+X_1$ and $C.r=-\tfrac1{12}$, $C.s=-\tfrac12$, $C.t=\tfrac1{24}$ as constant Laurent series; (iii) the Weierstrass curve of $r$, pushed forward along $K\to k_0((\mathsf q))$, equals $C\cdot\mathrm{tateBase}\,k_0\,(q\ell')$; (iv) for every prime factor $p$ of $M'$, every field $F'$, every ring homomorphism $f:k_0\to F'$ and every primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta'\in F'$, the polynomial $r.\mathrm{level}.1\,p$, pushed to $k_0((\mathsf q))$ and then to $F'((\mathsf q))$ coefficientwise along $f$, equals $\mathrm{kernelVariableChangeDeg}$ for the transported variable change and the degree $\mathrm{gamma0PowDeg}\,p\,v_p(M')$ applied to $\prod_a\bigl(X-(\mathrm{toricPoint}\,F'\,(q\ell')\,(\zeta'^a))_1\bigr)$, the product over $1\le a\le p^{v_p(M')}/2$ with $p\nmid a$; (v) the level-$\ell'$ datum $r.\mathrm{level}.2.1$, pushed to $k_0((\mathsf q))$, equals the $C$-variable change of $\mathrm{cuspData}\,k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta$ at the pair of vectors $(q,0)$ and $(0,-q)$ in $(\mathbb Z/q\ell')^2$; and (vi) there exist $P_x,P_y,Q_x,Q_y\in K$ whose images in $k_0((\mathsf q))$ are the four coordinates $x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q$ of the $C$-variable change of $\mathrm{cuspData}\,k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta$ at the vectors $(\ell',0)$ and $(0,-\ell')$, such that the Drinfeld sections $r.\mathrm{level}.2.2.P$ and $r.\mathrm{level}.2.2.Q$ pass through $(P_x,P_y)$ and $(Q_x,Q_y)$ respectively in the sense of `IsSectionThrough`.
--
--   *Conclusion.* For every nonzero natural number $d$ dividing $M'$, every witness that $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,(q\ell'd)$ lies in $K$, every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ equipped with compatible $A_0$- and $K$-algebra structures, every raw point $x_\Omega$ of $\mathcal R$ over $\Omega$ whose class is the image of $x_0$ under the structure map $K\to\Omega$, and every point $g$ of the affine curve underlying $x_\Omega$ such that ($hg$) $g$ has additive order exactly $M'$ and, for every prime factor $p$ of $M'$, every $n$ and every nonsingular affine point $(x_1,y_1)$ of that curve with $n\cdot g=(x_1,y_1)$ and $n\cdot g$ of order $p^{v_p(M')}$, the element $x_1$ is a root of the kernel polynomial $x_\Omega.\mathrm{level}.1\,p$, the image in $\Omega$ of $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,k_0\,(q\ell'd)\in K$ equals $\mathrm{cyclicQuotientJ}$ of the curve of $x_\Omega$ at the subgroup of integral multiples of $(M'/d)\cdot g$ and the integer $d$, namely $c_4^3/\Delta$ of the corresponding cyclic quotient curve.
--
--   This is the Tate-curve reading of the quotient $j$-invariant at the Tate point of the full-level rigid moduli datum: at the point corresponding to the Tate curve with its $\Gamma_0(M')$, level-$\ell'$ and Drinfeld level-$q$ structures, the $d$-isogenous $j$-invariant is the $\mathsf q$-expansion $j(\mathsf q^{q\ell'd})$, for every divisor $d$ of $M'$ and in every algebraically closed field over $K$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_classify_preimage_forall_apply_eq_cyclicQuotientJ_etale_of_tatePoint_gamma0Pow`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_classify_preimage_forall_apply_eq_cyclicQuotientJ_etale_of_tatePoint_gamma0Pow) in the construction of the integral model of the modular curve at $q$.
